All 16 functions from `KernSmooth/R/all.R` have been converted to Python and written to `ablation/conversion_guides/guides/4/all.R/`, in dependency order (leaves first):

1. `.onAttach`, `.onUnload` — no dependencies
2. `linbin`, `rlbin`, `linbin2D`, `blkest`, `cpblock` — leaf Fortran-wrapping functions, reimplemented in NumPy
3. `bkfe`, `locpoly`, `sdiag`, `sstdiag` — depend on `linbin`/`rlbin`
4. `bkde`, `bkde2D` — depend on `linbin`/`linbin2D`
5. `dpih`, `dpik` — depend on `bkfe`/`linbin`
6. `dpill` — depends on all six of `rlbin`, `cpblock`, `blkest`, `locpoly`, `sdiag`, `sstdiag`

Notable finds during conversion: the agent converting `locpoly` caught and fixed a real bug in the earlier `rlbin` conversion (it was missing a right-endpoint binning correction present in `KernSmooth/src/rlbin.f` but absent from `linbin.f` — I independently verified this against the Fortran sources). Several later conversions (`sdiag`, `bkde2D`, `dpill`) were validated numerically against the actual installed R `KernSmooth` package and matched to 10+ decimal places. No functions failed to convert.
